# f1 = pick, f2 = bits. Behind `i < bound`, bound from 0 to 3, the walk tells int[0, 2] of i, and `i < 3` is folded: no test, no other arm. In f2 `i >>> 0` hands i on, for it lies from 0 to 2.
# i is a raw int32: the name behind the test has no box, so the word that checks proofs (testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime, which --aot-harden=proof-checks stands for) asked nothing of it: a range that is false
# gave the other arm in silence. Now the range is held against the number where the fold is made. As users build, nothing is asked.
# RUN(HELD): walk f1 f2 mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# HELD-LABEL: code f1:Body
# HELD: = Narrowed
# HELD: CheckProof {{*}}checkedType=int[0, 2] typeWithoutProof=i32 source=Test
# HELD-LABEL: code f2:Body
# HELD: CheckProof {{*}}checkedType=int[0, 2] typeWithoutProof=i32 source=Test
# RUN(PLAIN): walk f1 f2 mechanisms=all
# PLAIN-NOT: source=Test
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# RIGHT: low out out 1 3
# RIGHT-NEXT: exited with 0
# THE LIE, testing=EqualIntegersAreTakenToBeLess: the code generator answers `<` of two integers as `<=`: 3 passes `i < bound` under a bound of 3, and the fold answers for it in silence.
# RUN(LIE): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=EqualIntegersAreTakenToBeLess
# LIE: low low out
# RUN(STOPPED): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime,EqualIntegersAreTakenToBeLess
# STOPPED: AOT: f{{*}} at {{*}}: what a test has let pass is not of the type that the compiler has proven of it
# STOPPED-NOT: low low
# STOPPED: crashed with
